#X connect 11 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X connect 3 0 18 0;
#X restore 1600 420 pd param-in;
#X text 20 580 EVERY BRANCH OUT OF route GOES THROUGH list append FIRST. When route matches \, whatever is left over comes out as a MESSAGE \, not a list -- so boot v0.2-ready arrives with v0.2-ready as its selector and alert warn u_init x arrives with warn as its selector. Feed that to a trigger and Pd says: can only convert 's' to 'b' or 'a'. list append turns it back into a list., f 58;
#X text 560 580 plan-conventions.md describes this for a remainder that is a SINGLE symbol. It is wider than that: any remainder whose first atom is a symbol arrives as a selector plus arguments. A remainder starting with a float -- in-l 43 dB -- really is a list \, which is why the level chain needs no list append., f 58;
#X text 1100 580 modal-off carries nothing \, so route hands out a bang and it goes straight to the layer logic. It is a reserved name rather than a modal with empty text because clearing a layer and setting one are different operations., f 58;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 1 4 1;
#X connect 3 2 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 3 3 7 0;
#X connect 3 4 8 0;
#X connect 3 5 9 0;
#X connect 3 6 10 0;
#X restore 20 420 pd disp-in;
#X text 180 424 the bus \, the reserved names \, and where a parameter comes from, f 40;
#N canvas 40 40 1500 900 layers 0;
#X text 20 20 ONE delay PER LAYER \, RETRIGGERED. A bang restarts delay \, so a moving control keeps param alive and the timer fires 1.2 s after the LAST message rather than the first., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 r \$0-on-param;
#X obj 20 170 t b b;
#X obj 200 220 1;
#X obj 20 220 del 1200;
#X obj 20 270 0;
#X obj 20 320 s \$0-a-param;
#X obj 500 130 r \$0-on-modal;
#X obj 500 170 t b b;
#X obj 680 220 1;
#X obj 500 220 del 30000;
#X obj 500 270 0;
#X obj 500 320 s \$0-a-modal;
#X obj 980 130 r \$0-on-alert;
#X obj 980 170 t b b;
#X obj 1160 220 1;
#X obj 980 220 del 2000;
#X obj 980 270 0;
#X obj 980 320 s \$0-a-alert;
#X obj 1160 170 r \$0-a-ttl;
#X obj 700 400 r \$0-off-modal;
#X obj 700 440 0;
#X obj 700 480 s \$0-a-modal;
#X text 20 560 home has no entry here. It is always active and never expires -- it is what the priority cascade falls through to \, so there is nothing to switch on or off., f 46;
#X text 400 560 modal's 30 s safety TTL is insurance \, not design. A lost modal-off on a device with no console would otherwise cover the screen until the patch was reloaded., f 46;
#X text 780 560 The alert TTL arrives on \$0-a-ttl BEFORE \$0-on-alert does \, because alert-in's trigger writes the fields before it raises the layer. Setting a delay's time does not start it., f 46;
#X text 1120 560 Retriggering is why the readout follows your hands: each new value pushes the expiry out again \, so the screen clears 1.2 s after you stop rather than 1.2 s after you start., f 46;
