#N canvas 40 40 1300 700 pick 0;
#X text 20 20 PRIORITY AS A CASCADE \, NOT ARITHMETIC. select 1 passes the non-matching 0 out of its right outlet \, so this reads top to bottom in exactly priority order. Adding a layer is one more link., f 58;
#X obj 20 130 inlet;
#X obj 20 190 f;
#X obj 140 150 r \$0-a-alert;
#X obj 20 240 select 1;
#X obj 20 300 outlet;
#X obj 340 190 f;
#X obj 460 150 r \$0-a-modal;
#X obj 340 240 select 1;
#X obj 340 300 outlet;
#X obj 660 190 f;
#X obj 780 150 r \$0-a-param;
#X obj 660 240 select 1;
#X obj 660 300 outlet;
#X obj 240 300 t b;
#X obj 560 300 t b;
#X obj 880 300 t b;
#X obj 980 380 outlet;
#X text 20 460 Outlet order is x position \, so left to right these are alert \, modal \, param \, home -- highest priority first \, which is also the order they are tested in., f 46;
#X text 420 460 f with no argument starts at 0 \, so before anything has ever fired \, every layer is down and home draws. That is the correct cold-start behaviour and it costs nothing., f 46;
#X text 820 460 The t b is not decoration: select's right outlet emits the float it did not match \, and f wants a bang on its hot inlet \, not a 0 -- a 0 would overwrite the flag it is about to read., f 46;
#X connect 3 0 2 1;
